d poll is too old and too heavily sponsored to move the forecast materially, leaving the Republican a clear but not insurmountable favorite.", "watch": "Traction from the Orlando Sentinel story about homes with possible subpar materials tied to a candidate-owned company, or a stronger-than-expected Democratic national wave, could tighten the race late.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 79 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Daniel Webster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 11th District leans Republican by about seven points, and Daniel Webster's retirement leaves an open seat. Republican Joe Strada enters the general election with an enormous financial edge, having raised more than $5.4 million and holding over $800,000 on hand, while Democrat James Pericola has raised roughly $197,000 with about $22,000 available. Outside spending has modestly favored Strada, and a Libertarian is also on the ballot.\n\nExpert raters overwhelmingly place the seat in the Solid or Safe Republican column, with a few calling it Likely or Lean Republican, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-ten chance. The only public poll, a July survey from a Democratic-sponsored firm, showed a 42-42 tie, but its age and sponsorship limit its weight. Strada also survived a narrow primary recount, suggesting some GOP fracture that has largely healed. District lean, money, and ratings make the Republican a clear favorite, though the open seat and a potentially favorable environment for Democrats leave room for a closer finish.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1071, "margin": -7.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1312, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1199, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0703, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0989, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1239, "mar